sentences.l:
To Teucer first and to Leitus he went, bidding them on,
and Peneleos the hero and Thoas and Deipyrus,
and Meriones and Antilochus, masters of the battle-cry;

which in the forest
of jackals and leopards and wolves are the food,
roaming idly, without might, nor is there joy of battle in them;

Nor would I, for my part,
fight with a man who should slacken from war,
grievous as he is;

for they, the best,
having been picked out, were awaiting the Trojans and Hector the heavenly,
having fenced spear with spear, shield with shield close-layered;

like a rolling boulder from a rock,
which down from the brow a winter-flowing river pushes,
having broken with boundless rain the supports of the shameless rock;

So Hector, while on the one hand he threatened that as far as the sea
easily he would go through the huts and the ships of the Achaeans,
killing;

and they, facing him, the sons of the Achaeans,
stabbing with swords and with double-pointed spears,
pushed him away from themselves;

And Meriones at him took aim with his shining spear,
and hit, and did not miss, the shield, equal on every side,
of bull’s hide;

and Deiphobus
held the bull’s-hide shield away from himself, and feared in his heart
the spear of Meriones, wise-minded;
